BFGS on Convex Nonsmooth Functions:
Convergence and Failure

Author-review edition, 13 September 2026



Abstract Ordinary full BFGS often succeeds on nonsmooth objectives, but
its convergence theory must accommodate gradient jumps and possible degen-
eration of the quasi-Newton matrices. We give complementary positive and
negative results for finite convex objectives with Armijo–weak-Wolfe steps and
actual gradients at differentiable accepted iterates. A centered smooth–sublinear
theorem proves global whole-sequence convergence for u2 + |v| and applies in
arbitrary dimension on compact initial sublevels. Coercive degree-one homo-
geneous objectives, including the stated max-linear and gauge families, admit
global convergence. Local results cover finite polyhedral smooth-piece parti-
tions and nondegenerate planar smooth maxima; an explicit curved-interface
example requires moving comparison geometry beyond the radial criterion. In
contrast, a fixed unit-first implementation can converge to nonstationary points
from starts arbitrarily close to the unique minimizer of one fixed objective.
One construction is a maximum of four globally convex C 2,1 branches; another
retains uniform strong convexity with C 1,1 branches that lose second differentia-
bility at the accumulation point. The positive proofs derive an adjacent-degree
hierarchy for actual principal minors of full-BFGS matrices, then combine
reciprocal growth with comparison energies. The resulting objective-level condi-
tions require neither iterate convergence nor prescribed spectral behavior. The
negative proofs certify the complete trial trees and infinite local embeddings.
These results expand the convergence theory while ruling out unrestricted
deterministic guarantees for the specified classes.
Keywords BFGS · convex nonsmooth optimization · global convergence ·
local convergence · counterexample
Mathematics Subject Classification (2020) 90C30 · 90C53 · 49J52


1 Introduction

BFGS builds its search directions from changes in gradients, yet it often works
well when those gradients jump. Classical convex convergence theory uses
smoothness together with the evolving quasi-Newton matrices [9,8,7]. Lewis–
Overton’s experiments and analyses [1] exposed the difficulty of extending that
theory: nonsmooth BFGS can approach a minimizer while its inverse-Hessian
approximations become highly singular. What global or local convergence can
2


ordinary full BFGS guarantee on convex nonsmooth functions, and how can one
prove it without assuming the asymptotic behavior of its iterates or matrices?
    We give three answers: deterministic counterexamples to every-start local
convergence; global and local convergence theorems for structured convex
families, including the mixed benchmark below; and a principal-minor and
comparison-energy argument that remains effective as the BFGS matrices
degenerate.
    Lewis–Overton’s Challenge 7.1 [1] gives this question a broader, probabilistic
setting. For locally Lipschitz semialgebraic objectives with bounded sublevels
and absolutely continuously distributed initial data, it asks about nonbreak-
down, stationary cluster points, R-linear convergence of all function trial values,
and identification of spectral subspaces under partly smooth convergence. We
study deterministic guarantees for convex objectives. Successful termination is
allowed; infinite runs are analyzed under the differentiable-iterate convention
made precise below.
    Substantial positive theory precedes this work. Guo–Lewis extends Powell’s
argument through smooth convex replacements on regions relevant to a BFGS
sequence [5, Theorems 3.2, 4.1 and 4.3]. The resulting theory proves Euclidean-
norm convergence in arbitrary finite dimension [5, Corollary 4.2]; for the mixed
model

                               F (u, v) = u2 + |v|

Corollary 4.4 gives only a subsequence approaching a point of v = 0. The
following paragraph explicitly leaves convergence of every BFGS sequence
to (0, 0) open [5, Corollary 4.4 and following discussion]. Approaching the
nonsmooth line does not force the smooth coordinate u to vanish. Corollary 2
answers this whole-sequence question for every admissible full-BFGS run, as a
consequence of an arbitrary-dimensional family theorem rather than a special
planar recurrence.
    Two other advances clarify the remaining task. Xie–Wächter’s two-piece
unbounded affine model leads to an unbounded descent direction after finitely
many iterations (Xie and Wächter, 2017 preprint, arXiv:1712.08571v1, Theorem
IV.1); a value-convergence theorem for a broader homogeneous family does
not replace that finite escape result. Gebken’s published 2026 introduction still
identifies u2 + |v| as unresolved. His local criticality theorem for continuous
smooth selections assumes an iterate limit, repeated activity, and specified
spectral and gradient-jump behavior [3, Theorem 3.1 and (B1)]. Our positive
results instead derive convergence from objective hypotheses within the convex
families stated here; they do not subsume his potentially nonconvex selection
setting.
    Throughout the paper, an admissible run uses the unmodified full BFGS
update, an arbitrary H0 ≻ 0, fixed 0 < c1 < c2 < 1, Armijo–weak-Wolfe steps,
and differentiable accepted points with nonzero gradients (Definition 1). The
positive theorems concern every infinite such run. They do not assert that
every search ray contains an acceptable differentiable point.
BFGS: convergence and failure                                                  3


Deterministic limits. Prescribed-orbit constructions give smooth nonconvex fail-
ures [14,15]; nonconvexity also separates the examples of Lewis–Zhang [12] and
Diao (Diao, 2026 preprint, arXiv:2609.09686v1) from the question here. Lewis–
Overton’s nonsmooth examples use exact search or subgradient choices (Lewis
and Overton, Behavior of BFGS with an Exact Line Search on Nonsmooth
Examples, 2008 manuscript), while Asl–Overton’s failure theorem concerns a
scaled memoryless update [13]. These cited examples do not supply convex
local failure with the full update and the specified search. We construct convex
objectives for which a fixed unit-first Armijo–weak-Wolfe doubling/bisection
search generates full-BFGS iterates converging to a nonstationary point. For
each local construction, one fixed objective has failing starts arbitrarily close
to its unique minimizer, all with H0 = I, and every accepted or rejected query
approaches that minimizer across the family of runs. One construction is a
maximum of four convex C 2,1 branches with bounded Hessians; another is
a maximum of four uniformly strongly convex C 1,1 branches, three of which
are not twice differentiable at the minimizer (Theorems 10 and 11). These
refute every-start neighborhood guarantees under either regularity regime.
They establish no positive-measure failure set and do not refute the almost-sure
semialgebraic challenge.


Global and local convergence. We prove global value convergence for centered
smooth-plus-sublinear objectives with compact initial sublevels contained in
the region where the representation holds, and whole-iterate convergence when
the minimizer is unique (Theorem 5). This settles the mixed benchmark above.
For coercive finite convex degree-one homogeneous functions, including coercive
maxima of finitely many linear functions, every admissible run converges to zero
(Theorem 6). This includes every norm, for example ∥x∥1 , whose nonsmooth
set for n ≥ 2 contains coordinate hyperplanes, beyond the isolated nonsmooth
point of the Euclidean-norm case above. Local results cover smooth convex plus
finite polyhedral terms and finite polyhedral partitions with smooth extensions
(Theorem 7). Moving comparison geometry also yields local convergence for
planar smooth maxima under strict-contour and active-gradient nondegeneracy
assumptions (Corollary 7). The explicit three-quadratic example in Theorem 9
has curved switching sets and fails the radial condition, so this extension has a
concrete payoff beyond inward radial modeling.


Analysis with degenerating matrices. The classical smooth analysis combines
trace and determinant identities in a matrix potential [7, Section 5]. The
uniform smooth secant bound used there need not hold when gradients jump.
We prove an inequality linking each principal-minor degree to the next for the
actual full-BFGS update. This adjacent-degree control forces the arithmetic
mean of the reciprocals 1/(gkT Hk gk ) to diverge when gradients are bounded and
predicted decreases tend to zero. Comparison energies turn this restriction into
convergence: a field must retain information about the objective gap, control
the gradient changes it sees, and move with a controlled energy cost. The field
4


is an analytical device, not part of the algorithm. Neither a bounded condition
number nor a prescribed spectral limit is needed (Theorems 1–3).
    Our conditions are sufficient; they do not classify every successful run.
Sections 2 and 3 explain and establish the mechanism; Sections 4 and 5
prove its global and local consequences, and Section 6 gives the deterministic
counterexamples. The article includes the essential proofs of these claims.
An independent complete companion preserves the wider noncompact and
quantitative theory, with Lean proofs, exact correspondence and reproducible
audit evidence.



2 What the results say about nonsmooth BFGS

The mixed ridge F (u, v) = u2 + |v| isolates the difficulty left by a subsequence
approaching the nonsmooth line. Reaching v = 0 does not eliminate the
horizontal error u2 . Yet the vertical gradient jump does not remove the direction
that can reduce this error. Along v = 0 the limiting gradients are (2u, 1) and
(2u, −1). Their difference is vertical. At a point with u ̸= 0, the horizontal
vector (−u, 0) has the same strictly negative pairing −2u2 with both gradients.
The inward radial model also has a small error:

