    The right panel asks a different question: can a genuine full-BFGS run
contract around a point where descent still exists? The bad corner is specific.
Its slopes are g0 = (1, 0), g1 = (−1, −1/8) and g2 = (c◦ , d◦ ), where (c◦ , d◦ ) is
defined exactly in Appendix C. They are affinely independent, so no nonzero
direction pairs equally with all three. Yet (−1, 16) has negative pairing with
each, and zero is outside their convex hull. The corner is therefore nonstationary.
Section 6 constructs a three-step cycle and verifies that the stated line search
produces it, including all rejected trials. This additional dynamical proof is
what makes the corner a counterexample.
    This distinction between affine and convex balance helps interpret the
examples. For a nonempty finite family of slopes ai , equality aTi v = κ < 0
for every i asks for a common rate of descent, whereas aTi v < 0 asks only for
descent. The former fails when zero belongs to their affine hull; the latter fails
when zero belongs to their convex hull. Indeed, a linear functional constant on
the slopes is constant on their affine hull. Conversely, if zero is outside that
affine hull, its nearest point gives a separating functional constant on the hull;
changing its sign gives the negative common value. The convex version follows
from the same closest-point argument for the convex hull. These are elementary
separation facts, not a classification of BFGS behavior.
