6


General affine offsets can place the bad corner away from the true optimizer,
whereas homogeneity centers all linear pieces at the origin.
    Local behavior raises a further distinction. Finitely many smooth formulas
can reproduce bad configurations at infinitely many shrinking scales. A finite
polyhedral partition near the optimizer controls the inward model because
each remaining cell contains the optimizer after localization. Curved interfaces
require a different argument: the planar example in Section 5 has a radial
obstruction, yet a moving contour field proves convergence. These are com-
plementary sufficient mechanisms and specific realized failures, rather than a
classification by jump span.


3 The analytical framework

Smooth convergence proofs can relate a gradient difference to curvature along
a short step. A nonsmooth secant can instead contain a jump that remains
large as the step shrinks. Our starting point is to measure how much of that
secant is seen by a direction that still detects the objective gap. A quadratic
comparison energy separates the cost of this component from a negative term
measuring missed progress. The matrix hierarchy then shows that persistent
missed progress cannot be paid for by a bounded secant cost per iteration. The
argument uses the matrices actually generated by BFGS throughout.

Definition 1 (Admissible runs) Let F : Rn → R be finite and convex, with
n ≥ 1. An admissible run is an infinite sequence of differentiable accepted
points xk , nonzero gradients gk = ∇F (xk ), and matrices generated by

