3.2 Moving comparison directions

The radial direction is effective for a centered homogeneous term, but a curved
switching interface may rotate the direction in which its jump cancels. The
comparison geometry must then move. Three requirements have distinct roles.
Visibility keeps the remaining objective gap detectable on a positive band.
Secant control limits the gradient-jump component seen by the geometry at the
next endpoint. Transport bounds the cost of changing that geometry between
endpoints. The identity B bk sk = 0 means that movement parallel to the executed
step carries no cost in the part of the update left after its negative rank-one
term. Only the perpendicular projection needs control.
    A positive-semidefinite field packages one or several comparison directions.
It is constructed from the objective for the analysis; BFGS neither evaluates it
nor changes its step in response to it. The rank-one radial field in Section 4
and the contour field in Section 5 illustrate the two designs.
    For differentiable x, z, write g = ∇F (x), h = ∇F (z), s = z − x, y = h − g,
d = −g T s, ∆ = F (x) − F (z) and t = y T s. Call the pair eligible when

                     d > 0,        c1 d ≤ ∆ ≤ d,      t ≥ ωd.

All accepted pairs are eligible. Requiring a property of every eligible pair is an
objective assumption; it does not assert that an arbitrary sequence of eligible
pairs can be realized by BFGS. Put Πs = I − ssT /(sT s).

Definition 2 (Band certificate) Let K = {F ≤ b} be compact, with b >
F∗ = min F . For each nonempty Kη = {x ∈ K : F (x) ≥ F∗ + η}, η > 0, a
band certificate consists of a finite-valued symmetric PSD field Wη (x) on its
differentiable points and constants mη > 0, Cη ≥ 0, Lη ≥ 0, δη > 0 such that

