5.2 A moving direction for curved planar switching

The radial direction can fail for a different reason: a curved interface may carry
a gradient jump with a nonzero radial component. The secant correction then
blows up as endpoints approach opposite sides of the interface. The transported
framework allows the comparison direction to follow the interface instead. It
must first remain visible to every local supporting model, then cancel the
leading jump, and finally move at a cost controlled by the actual objective
decrease. The field is constructed from the objective; BFGS never evaluates it.
BFGS: convergence and failure                                                  19


   For F : R2 → R, write


                            Caff = {p : 0 ∈ aff ∂F (p)}.


At a point outside this set, let a be the least-norm point of aff ∂F (p). Or-
thogonality to its direction space gives g T a = ∥a∥2 for every g ∈ ∂F (p). Thus
−a/∥a∥2 decreases all supporting models at the same unit rate. Affine criticality
concerns failure of this common-rate direction; ordinary stationarity concerns
failure of every strict common descent direction. They need not coincide.
    The two planar hypotheses have separate roles. Strictly convex positive
sublevels prohibit long accepted chords with vanishing value decrease. Regular
two-branch switching makes each positive-level corner move smoothly with
the level and supplies a direction paired equally with both gradients. Neither
condition imposes a matrix limit or a lower bound on all eigenvalues of a
Hessian. A common positive lower Hessian bound is one sufficient way to
obtain strict contours, but is not required.
    Here is the construction near a compact noncritical part of a positive level.
At a two-branch corner, the independent gradients let us solve f1 (pj (t)) =
f2 (pj (t)) = t for the corner as the level varies. Its velocity vj (t) = p′j (t)
satisfies ∇fi (pj (t))T vj (t) = 1 for both branches. Thus −vj is an equal-rate
descent direction, while vj is the positively paired field used in the energy.
There are finitely many such corners in the compact part under consideration.
Their supporting-normal arcs are disjoint: a common normal at two corners
would expose the whole segment between them, contrary to the strict-contour
assumption. Their angular separation leaves room to join the local velocities
smoothly.
    The joining also has to control derivatives. For unit normal n(θ) =
(cos θ, sin θ) and tangent T (θ) = (− sin θ, cos θ), choose a positive angular
function h(t, θ) that equals n(θ)T vj (t) near the jth normal arc. Disjoint
smooth cutoffs blend these positive pairings with h = 1 elsewhere. The field
w = hn + hθ T , evaluated at t = F (x) and the gradient’s normal angle, equals
vj (F (x)) on both sides of that corner. Its angular derivative is (h + hθθ )T :
the normal terms cancel. Consequently Dw preserves the tangent line of each
smooth level. The one-dimensional tangent is essential here: for a tangent step
s, this makes Dw s parallel to s, so det(s, Dw s) = 0.
    For a sufficiently short eligible chord, weak Wolfe makes det(s, w) uniformly
nonzero relative to ∥s∥. Tangent preservation then bounds the change of
log | det(s, w)| by a constant times the variation of F along the chord. Convexity
bounds that variation by 2d, giving the transport factor | log λ| ≤ Cd. Since
Armijo gives d ≤ (F (x) − F (z))/c1 , transport is charged to summable objective
decreases; finite total path length is not required. Appendix B supplies the
cutoffs, estimates through switching points, and secant bound. These yield one
field on a neighborhood of the compact level set, not just separate directions
at its corners.
