6 Failure: a contracting mechanism and local realizations

The positive theorems identify geometry that permits convergence. We now
show why convexity and regularity of the branch formulas cannot supply an
unrestricted deterministic replacement. The first tempting assertion is global:
compact sublevels might seem enough to prevent convergence to a wrong corner.
The second is local: for one finite maximum of very smooth convex branches,
perhaps every sufficiently close start succeeds. We construct an actual failure of
each assertion, and then examine what changes when positive lower curvature
is imposed.
24


    The mechanism has three parts. A three-step BFGS cycle returns the
inverse matrix to a smaller multiple of itself while approaching a nonstationary
corner. Strict function and gradient inequalities force the specified line search
to produce that cycle. Finally, local copies of the corner accumulate at the true
minimizer of one objective. Matching spatial and objective scales keeps the
initial matrix fixed across the copies. The distinction between a formal cycle
and an implemented run matters at every part of this argument.


