matrix is then used in every copy; one fixed affine change makes it I. The faster
variation of the scalar derivative retains global C 1,1 regularity but prevents
second differentiability at the accumulation point. This is why the theorem
does not supply a finite strongly convex C 2 local counterexample.
    The two constructions separate upper branch regularity from positive lower
curvature. Their common mechanism is a local copy of an actual bad query
tree; the different scalar scales determine which regularity survives at the
accumulation point. The remaining convergence questions are discussed next.


7 Synthesis, verification and the remaining questions

The mixed benchmark u2 + |v| has a whole-sequence convergence answer within
an arbitrary-dimensional centered-family theorem. Finite polyhedral geometry
supplies a local inward model, while a moving field handles the three-quadratic
example’s curved switches. In each case comparison energies use the principal-
minor hierarchy to detect a remaining objective gap.

Why the counterexamples evade these hypotheses. The distinctions can be read
from the constructed objectives. The homogeneous seed satisfies G(t(−1, 16)) =
−t, so it is unbounded below; its coercive cap contains the affine term v − 17
and is not homogeneous. Neither belongs to the coercive homogeneous class
of Theorem 6. In the local embeddings, wrong vertices recur at every scale
around the same minimizer. They cannot be removed by taking a smaller
neighborhood, and the resulting switching geometry is not resolved by a finite
local polyhedral-cell partition as in Theorem 7. Finitely many smooth formulas
do not by themselves supply that partition.
    There is also a direct test of the planar hypotheses. In (32), all four branches
are active at zero, with indexed gradients 0, 0, 0, e2 , where e2 = (0, 1)T . Equa-
tion (54) gives the same pattern before whitening: the common quadratic has
zero gradient at zero, and ψb′ (0) = 0. After the one fixed change x = Az, the
34


gradients are 0, 0, 0, AT e2 , with AT e2 ̸= 0. Thus the repeated zero gradients
violate affine independence, and a combination with positive weight λ3 on
the fourth branch equals λ3 e2 ̸= 0 (or λ3 AT e2 ̸= 0 after whitening). The
strictly positive optimal weights in Corollary 7 therefore fail as well. The lower-
curvature branches also lose second differentiability at zero. Positive strong
convexity alone supplies neither the missing regularity nor this nondegeneracy.
    The secant calculation explains how curvature and the failure mechanism
coexist. For Qµ (x) = µ∥x∥2 /2 + G(x), suppose the differentiable endpoints x
and x + s have active slopes gi and gj . Then exactly
                                                
                y = µ(x + s) + gj − µx + gi = µs + (gj − gi ).                 (35)
Along the certified perturbed orbit, s → 0 while the three distinct slopes
continue to cycle. The positive-curvature contribution therefore vanishes with
the step, while the branch jumps persist. The stable-manifold and strict-
query arguments establish that this decomposition is realized by the specified
algorithm; a branch change alone does not imply failure.
    These comparisons explain sufficient hypotheses separating the proved
positive classes from the examples. They do not make affine independence,
positive multipliers or a comparison certificate necessary for convergence,
and they do not turn every nonoptimal affine-critical point or accumulating
singularity into a counterexample. The broader local question for finitely many
uniformly strongly convex C 2 branches remains open here. The examples
concern the specified weak-Wolfe implementation and exceptional deterministic
starts; they do not settle Lewis–Overton’s almost-sure challenge under its
additional hypotheses [1, Challenge 7.1].

