D Positive lower curvature through a perturbed cycle

Theorem 11 uses a different regularity balance from Theorem 10. Adding a
positive quadratic changes the seed orbit, so exact homogeneity alone no longer
supplies a trajectory. We first prove persistence on a stable manifold and then
copy one entire perturbed orbit at all scales. The first step yields a global
failure from a suitable matrix; the copying step makes bad starts approach
the minimizer of one fixed objective; a final fixed change of coordinates makes
their common initial matrix the identity. The price of retaining a positive lower
curvature bound is the loss of second differentiability of the branches at the
accumulation point. Throughout this appendix the line-search constants are
c1 = 10−4 and c2 = 1/2.


D.1 A strongly convex global example

A common positive quadratic leaves the active-branch geometry unchanged
and makes every sublevel compact. It perturbs the gradients, however, so the
homogeneous seed is no longer an exact orbit. Close to the corner, the quadratic
gradient is small relative to the fixed slopes. The task is to turn this smallness
into an exact infinite trajectory, with every rejection and acceptance retained.
    Use the fixed slopes of Appendix C and let

