Proof (Proposition 1) The inverse function theorem makes the return map a
local diffeomorphism. The classical local stable-manifold theorem provides a
two-dimensional smooth stable manifold through P∗ , tangent to the stable
eigenspace; Dyatlov’s local-coordinate formulation and graph-transform proof
apply after an adapted splitting and rescaling (Dyatlov, Notes on hyperbolic
dynamics, 2018 preprint, arXiv:1805.11660v1, Section 3.5, Theorem 3 and
Remark 4; Section 3.1). By the last assertion of Lemma 9, this manifold
contains points with arbitrarily small positive τ . Choose such a point whose
forward return orbit stays in the implementation neighborhood and tends to
P∗ . Shrinking the neighborhood keeps r > 0 and M (b, e) ≻ 0, so τ + = τ r
preserves positivity. The actual intermediate matrices remain positive definite
by positive curvature.
    The physical iterates are x3m = τm zm → 0. Intermediate points and all
queries are τm times bounded continuous functions of the normalized state,
so they tend to zero too. Their normalized objective values stay positive by
the seed’s strict positivity. Armijo makes the values decrease to zero, which
exceeds (48). More explicitly, each of the finitely many physical candidate maps
has the form Φq (w) =Tτ q(w) and satisfies Φq (P∗ ) = 0. Choose the normalized
neighborhood inside q Φ−1   q (V ), then take a positive-scale local stable orbit
that stays in this neighborhood. Every physical query lies in V . This proves
the proposition. The initial matrix is supplied by the stable-manifold point,
not presumed to be identity or obtained by rounded forward shooting.
