D.2 An accumulation embedding with four strongly convex branches

The previous proposition places a bad orbit near its wrong corner, whereas the
desired local counterexample needs bad starts near the minimizer. We therefore
place shrinking copies around centers x̄ℓ converging to that minimizer. The
spatial factor will be aℓ , and the objective factor a2ℓ , which preserves the same
inverse matrix by (28). To accommodate the common quadratic, the three
scalar branch slopes are shifted so that the quadratic cross term restores the
original seed gradients inside every copy.
    The scalar interpolation must have value a2ℓ and derivative aℓ at its ℓth
node, with an affine neighborhood wide enough to contain all seed queries after
scaling. A monotone cubic ramp between these plateaux gives a convex function
with Lipschitz derivative. The node slopes divided by the node positions stay of
order one, so this construction need not, and will not, have a second derivative
at zero.
